--- !u!114 &4041452524342900699
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 4041452524342900696}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 3ff6acaa8b9f1994499dd387e6188532, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  Comments: "This demonstrates VR 360 stereoscopic rendering (left and right eye)
    using 2 cameras with post processing effects applied in a second pass. Every
    frame, both cameras are rendered to cubemaps and combined into a top-bottom equirectangular
    layout, which is then rendered by the post process camera for final output.\n\nWhy
    Two Camera Passes?\nSince post processing effects often introduce visible seams
    in cubemaps, this setup overcomes that by re-rendering the final output with
    a separate camera to apply post processing. \n\n360 Render Without Post Processing?\nThere
    is no setup for plain 360 rendering without post processing due to an internal
    gamma issue which slows down rendering considerably to correct. This is resolved
    by using the 2-pass camera method, so if you wish to render without post processing,
    simply use this setup but deactivate the post processing volume.\n\nTo preserve
    HDR quality floating space render textures are recommended.\n\nFor use in production
    the interocular distance (between cameras) and other settings may need to be
    adjusted to your project. The interocular distance is set by slightly shifting
    the camera positions. This is to simulate the distance between human eyes for
    a natural feel. This is usually between 50 and 75mm. For this example, 60mm is
    used which provides an interocular distance in meters of 0.06. This is applied
    to the left camera only to leave the right camera centered, since most people
    are right-eye dominant. This allows the right side to be used for standard flatscreen
    video and maintain centering for titles."
  Warning: 'Certain rendering features such as screen space effects, some shaders,
    and post processing effects may not be supported in stereo rendering setups.
    This can result in rendering anomalies between left and right eye. To avoid these
    issues, test early on and decide on rendering features that do work with multi-eye
    rendering. Also refer to Unity''s documentation for single and multipass rendering.


    Layer
    Masks

    The built-in ''Ignore Raycast'' layer is used to separate the rendering
    of the MainCamera from the post processing camera, volume, and image plane. Note
    also that the MainCamera exlcudes the ''Ignore Raycast'' layer in its culling
    mask so that it doesn''t render the image plane.


    You may change the layer
    to any that works best for your project, though make sure that both the Culling
    Mask and Volume Mask on the cameras are set to match the layer assigned, otherwise
    you may not get the results desired.'
  URLTitle: Render To Disk Documentation
  URL: https://axongenesis.gitbook.io/timeflow/reference/behaviors/rendering/render-to-disk
  URL2Title: Stereo Rendering
  URL2: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/SinglePassStereoRendering.html
